Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Adapted from the novel
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 5/15/2025
Notes: Photorealistic live-action; Midwest working-class palette; narrator name withheld until final beat

FADE IN:

EXT. KIRK STREET BRICK APARTMENT - DAY

A big brick fifth-floor walk-up. Alley dumpsters below. Cold Milwaukee light.

(SOUND: distant traffic, wind off the lake)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Big brother was mine. Not that stupid book. My real Big Brother.

INT. MOXIE'S GYM - DAY

Harsh fluorescents. Sweat and iron. A GUY curls a 40-pound bar. NICK enters: tall, heavily muscled, scars on chin and over left eyebrow, reddish-brown messy hair, pox-marked face, black intense eyes. Work boots, jeans, t-shirt. People move.

(SOUND: weights clank, heavy breath)

The guy drops the bar and offers the bench. Nick nods, takes a 50-pound bar for warm-ups.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick had a pair on him the size of steel wrecking balls. Face like a puzzle held with glue-gun sticky stuff.

INT. KIRK STREET FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT MILWAUKEE - DAY

Cramped living room. MA (aging, tired brown eyes, brown hair, house dress, afghan) sits near the TV. NARRATOR (average build, ordinary working-class face, worn t-shirt, jeans) hauls grocery bags. Nick loads six bags like a strongman.

(SOUND: TV game-show chatter, floorboards creak)

NICK
You got to keep an eye on her. She shouldn't be sleeping in the middle of the day.

NARRATOR
I will. I promise.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
We lived downtown after dad took off when I was eight. Fifth-floor walk-up. Pain in the ass.

EXT. WERNER STREET - DAY

Nick's 1980 Pontiac Trans Am: burnt orange, yellow flames, eagle on the hood, T-top. He leans on it, keys in hand, brown suede jacket.

(SOUND: V8 rumble idle, street noise)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He got a great deal. Twelve grand. People always look up when he pulls to the lights.

INT. METAL FACTORY - DAY

Assembly line. Big metal pieces. Crane lifts overhead. Nick runs the line, boots planted, hair helter-skelter.

(SOUND: machinery roar, metal clang, drill press whine)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He said it's pretty easy for the money. He comes over a lot. Feels like he still lives here.

INT. KIRK STREET FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT MILWAUKEE - NIGHT

Ma in her chair, afghan, glassy eyes. Pill bottles lined on the counter. Narrator in rumpled t-shirt watches her.

(SOUND: TV low, fridge hum)

MA
Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta there?

NARRATOR
Ma, we don't go to church anymore. Your pills are right in front of you.

She snaps back, empty look fading.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Somebody has to be here with Ma. She's getting older. Hard for her to see.

INT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

Warm oven glow. Counter phone. Narrator in apron, flour-dusted t-shirt, spins dough. Window on the street.

(SOUND: oven doors, dough slap, street traffic)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I can't afford school. Nick says you don't need a degree. All you need is to know somebody.

NICK (O.S.)
You're not gonna be a doctor. Hell, you're too old anyway.

EXT. PIZZA PARLOR SIDEWALK - DAY

Cement. Narrator steps out. SIONNA (short brown hair, pale English-story skin, lightest watery blue eyes, black short-sleeved V-neck, jeans, blue tennis shoes, backpack, fish-symbol necklace) trips and brushes his shoulder.

(SOUND: shoe scuff, city ambient)

NARRATOR
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

She brushes her shirt. They look at each other.

NARRATOR
I work here. What is your name?

SIONNA
Sionna.

NARRATOR
Sionna? That's different. What does that mean?

SIONNA
Irish goddess. River Shannon. If you want coffee sometime, you can call me.

She gives a number. Walks away smooth as waves, glances back over her shoulder.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was like I knew her. Intense. Like tides coming in and I didn't want to move my feet.

INT. KIRK STREET FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT MILWAUKEE - NIGHT

Ma mumbles, searching. Narrator finds the day's pill box.

(SOUND: bottles rattle, distant siren)

MA
I can't find my pain pills. Did we go to church?

NARRATOR
They're right here, Ma.

He grabs his coat and leaves.

EXT. PUBLIC LIBRARY - NIGHT

Big windows, old brick. Narrator hurries in from cold.

(SOUND: door heavy close, wind)

INT. PUBLIC LIBRARY - NIGHT

Wood floors a hundred years old. Lights on metal chains. Classic literature stacks. Desk lady. Narrator writes on a temporary card form.

(SOUND: pages turn, quiet footsteps, chain lights hum)

NARRATOR
How do I take a book home?

DESK LADY
Library card?

He gets the temporary paper. Pulls Wuthering Heights. Thin smile from the lady.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Who needed school when everything is right here free?

INT. KIRK STREET FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT MILWAUKEE - NIGHT

Ma already in bed. Narrator reads by lamp: Heathcliff and Catherine.

(SOUND: page turns, building pipes)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was a little like a soap opera. These two really loved each other. Reminded me of Sionna.

INT. JAVA HUT COFFEE SHOP - DAY

College crowd, backpacks, textbooks. Small table near back wall. Sionna in washed jeans, soft leather shoes, long-sleeved loose green top with hood. Watery blue eyes. Narrator sits opposite, cleaner long-sleeve.

(SOUND: espresso machine hiss, low student talk, cup clink)

SIONNA
What are you reading right now?

NARRATOR
Wuthering Heights. Guy and girl grew apart but were really in love.

SIONNA
I know. I read it.

She talks symbols: ghosts, double characters, moors. Wild unpredictable infertile. Love that never changes destroys.

SIONNA
A love that never changes will destroy you.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Something inside me was coming awake. She made me feel smarter just by being around her.

They plan dinner Friday.

EXT. DECIDUOUS FOREST - DREAM - DAY

Lush green. Moss on oak and maple. Wood sorrel, trillium, bleeding hearts. Narrator in white ballooning sleeves, embroidered red tunic, brown hose, rides close behind a woman with long brown hair on a dappled mare. Rose and warm grass scent.

(SOUND: hooves soft on forest floor, birds, wind in leaves)

They emerge to open field. He helps her down. First kiss. Her eyes lightest blue like early morning.

NARRATOR (DREAM)
(embracing)
I surrender completely.

INT. KIRK STREET FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT MILWAUKEE - NIGHT

Narrator bolts upright in bed, sweating, chest pounding.

(SOUND: bed springs, his sharp breath, cold room silence)

NARRATOR
Hold!

NARRATOR (V.O.)
What the hell was that? Clothes like old kings. It was Sionna. Did I insert her into the book?

INT. YOGA STUDIO - DAY

Purple rectangle mats. Twenty people. Narrator in t-shirt and shorts, shirt falling over face until he tucks it. Sionna beside him in stretch clothes. Downward dog, breath work.

(SOUND: instructor soft counts, collective breath, mat rustle)

He struggles, dizzy, groins screaming. After savasana he lies calm, eyes soft.

SIONNA
You were really great at that.

NARRATOR
I felt so dumb.

SIONNA
Your practice is your practice. Pretty soon you won't notice anyone else.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
After, nothing bothered me. Not pizzas. Not asshole customers. Just calm.

INT. KIRK STREET FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT MILWAUKEE - DAY

Nick stomps in, boots, brown suede jacket, face red, fists clenching. Ma in chair. Narrator freezes.

(SOUND: door slam, boots heavy, TV)

NICK
Ma missed her doctor's appointment yesterday, you ass. Where the hell were you?

NARRATOR
I totally forgot. I was with someone. I met a girl.

NICK
A girl. That's worth all this. Try looking at your priorities, Bro.

Nick turns, slams the door.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I hardly ever get into it with Nick. You just don't know what he is going to do.

INT. DOCTOR'S OFFICE / HOSPITAL ER - DAY

Dr. Carson in white coat, serious eyes, paper pad. Narrator stands. Ma in gown holding her chest.

(SOUND: monitor beep, hallway pager, fluorescent hum)

DR. CARSON
Your mother's blood pressure is dangerously high. Risk of stroke. Take her directly to the emergency room.

NARRATOR
Whoa man. Are you sure?

DR. CARSON
Confused. Chest pressure. We admit her for observation. IV medicine until stabilized.

Nick arrives later, pissed. They load Ma.

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - NIGHT

Ma dozing. Narrator asleep in the chair. Dream bleeds:

EXT. BATTLEFIELD - DREAM - DAY

Chaos. Yelling. Blood on ground. Blades. Narrator stands over a dying man. Blood bubbles.

DYING MAN
Save her.

Nick runs toward him, eyes fire, longest knife.

NICK (DREAM)
That you are, little brother.

He stabs. Narrator gasps awake in the hospital chair, heart pounding.

(SOUND: hospital monitor steady, his gasp, distant code)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick had killed me. Feeling of betrayal by my closest family. Worst of all.

INT. KIRK STREET FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT MILWAUKEE - NIGHT

Ma home. Blue weekly pill container on table. Nick in overstuffed brown chair, boots on footstool, beet-red face, hair wild. Narrator hands him the container.

(SOUND: TV cop show, container plastic rattle)

NICK
Aren't you nurse Nancy! How did you come up with this?

NARRATOR
Sionna helped me. She's really smart.

NICK
This chick sure has you wrapped around her finger.

NARRATOR
You haven't even met her. Give her a break!

NICK
Don't you talk to me like that, you little bastard.

NARRATOR
I'm not a bastard!

Nick slaps him across the head. Ear rings. Nick stomps out, door slam.

(SOUND: open-hand slap, ear ring, door crash)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In that split second I knew I never wanted Sionna to meet that prick.

EXT. GRADE SCHOOL YARD - DAY - FLASHBACK

Late 70s. Lilac bushes. YOUNG NARRATOR (grade-school jeans, t-shirt) and BOBBY doubled over laughing. JENNIFER with braids and red bows glares. YOUNG NICK (big sixth-grader, messy hair, school clothes) walks up.

(SOUND: kids yelling, recess bell distant)

YOUNG NARRATOR
Learn some responsibility!

MRS. STARK strides over, glasses on beaded necklace.

MRS. STARK
Mr. Olsen! Explain yourself before I send you to Mr. Pauls.

YOUNG NICK
He didn't mean anything. I'll talk to him. He'll never act up again.

MRS. STARK
It's too bad your brother doesn't have the same level head. I'll let it pass this time.

Later, bedroom. Young Nick musses the boy's hair.

YOUNG NICK
I'll always take care of you. Don't forget that.

YOUNG NARRATOR
I won't, Nick.

INT. KIRK STREET FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT MILWAUKEE - NIGHT

Present. Nick visits. Ma on cop show. Narrator mentions school.

(SOUND: TV, keys in basket)

NARRATOR
I'm going to community college. Bachelor's in business. Student loan.

NICK
Whoa. This chick telling you what to do? It ain't gonna be me helping when you finish with huge debt.

NARRATOR
It would mean a lot if you could be on my side for once. You are my brother.

NICK
Bros before ho's. You get me?

Narrator forces a smile.

NARRATOR
Sure, Nick. I get you.

INT. JOE'S BAR AND GRILL - NIGHT

Loud rock. Pool room left, quarters stacked. Bar right packed. Smoke. Waitresses in jeans and white blouses. Nick, BOB (casual jeans, t-shirt), SAMMY, Narrator with Bud. AC/DC on speakers.

(SOUND: AC/DC "Have a Drink on Me", pool crack, bar roar, bottles)

Nick spots LINDSEY with a dark-haired man. Approaches. Tension. Room quiets.

NICK
What's up, Lindsey? Who's this asshole?

DARK-HAIRED MAN
You got a problem with me? You're in my face like a little bitch!

Nick's nostrils flare, fists clench. Narrator steps in front.

NARRATOR
Nick, leave it. You don't even know this guy.

NICK
Keep out of it! You're next if you don't get out of my face.

Nick's right fist draws back in slow motion toward Narrator's face. JOE and BOUNCER rush in. Head-butt. Chaos. Narrator runs.

(SOUND: fist whoosh cut short, head-butt thud, shouts, door bang, running feet on pavement)

EXT. STREET - NIGHT

Narrator runs hard, chest exploding, cold air in lungs, to Sionna's.

(SOUND: boots pounding, heartbeat in ears, night traffic)

INT. SIONNA DUPLEX - NIGHT

Green suede sofa. Gray cat. Lamp. Sionna opens. Narrator collapses into her. She strokes his face.

(SOUND: door close, cat soft mew, his ragged breath)

SIONNA
Your brother tried to hit you. No one who really cares does that.

NARRATOR
He does care. I can't just ignore him.

SIONNA
You are becoming a bravely compassionate being. You can't let him stop you from your purpose.

NARRATOR
What is my purpose?

SIONNA
To find the true self within you and honor it.

They sleep on the sofa. Dream:

EXT. LAKE SHORE - DREAM - NIGHT

Black and white then color. Narrator exhausted soldier. Woman rises from water: long brown hair, light blue eyes, white gown sleeves like wings. Nick giant with mace from woods. She spreads arms; invisible force pushes Nick back until he shrinks and vanishes. They embrace.

(SOUND: wind howl, water lap, mace chain rattle, then silence)

INT. SIONNA DUPLEX - NIGHT

They bolt awake 3 a.m. on the sofa. Shuffle to queen bed, crisp sheets.

(SOUND: clock tick, sheets rustle)

SIONNA
Let's get some sleep. We've had an interesting day.

INT. KIRK STREET FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT MILWAUKEE - DAY

Months later montage feel. Sionna cooks butternut squash casserole. Reads to Ma. Pill container filled. Narrator studies at table, exhausted but purposeful. Nick absent.

(SOUND: oven timer, page turns, Ma soft laugh, pencil on paper)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Ma saw me as the leader now. Sionna became family. Every day I woke with purpose.

EXT. CHURCH / STREET - DAY

Nick (heavy coat) helps Ma in modest church coat into a car. He has had three Jack and Cokes. Drives.

(SOUND: car door, engine, then SCREECH, metal crunch, glass explode)

Crash. Silence. Later: cops, toxicology pending. Narrator in empty Kirk apartment stares at Ma's chair.

(SOUND: empty room hum, then his raw scream)

NARRATOR
Die you fucking jackass. Die!

He collapses crying, pulling hair. Sionna holds him.

INT. FUNERAL HOME / EXT. CATHOLIC CEMETERY ST. MARY'S - DAY

Modest maple casket. Few people. Father Joseph in clerical collar. Graveside hilltop near trees. Narrator throws handful of dirt. Thunk.

(SOUND: dirt on wood muted thunk, soft sobs, wind in trees)

FATHER JOSEPH
I have fought the good fight...

Flat stone later: Olivia Anne Olsen. August 28, 1933 to September 7, 1998. 2 Timothy 4:7.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I was the only son there. Rightly so. He killed her.

INT. HOSPITAL ICU - DAY

Nick in gown, chest tube to plastic box, oxygen, IV, right leg huge under bedspread. Sallow, black-purple bruises turning green, hair sticking up. Weak voice. Narrator stands cold.

(SOUND: monitor beep, oxygen hiss, hallway cart)

NICK
I guess I really fucked up. Can't believe Ma's gone.

Tears. Narrator stares.

NARRATOR
They took care of it. She's at peace. Plot at the top of the hill.

NICK
Cops say vehicular manslaughter. I was almost killed too.

NARRATOR
Whose fault was it, Nick?

He turns and walks out.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I no longer gave two shits about Nick.

EXT. CATHOLIC CEMETERY ST. MARY'S - DAY

Narrator places golden mums in inverted metal vase. Late fall sun. Bike nearby.

(SOUND: wind, distant traffic, water pour from bottle)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I hope she's finally happy. Leave the work and worry to me.

INT. PIZZA PARLOR - NIGHT

Tony (work shirt, keys) throws car keys. Narrator delivers fast, returns warm by ovens. Tommy struggles with map.

(SOUND: oven roar, phone ring, door chime)

TONY
Man, that was quick. How would you like to do more deliveries?

NARRATOR
Sure.

TONY
I'm sorry for your ma. You look tired. I worry.

Narrator nods. Surrogate father.

INT. SIONNA DUPLEX - NIGHT

Narrator dreams: farm sun, chickens, dogs, Sionna's dad pot smoke, mom handing Tupperware. Pregnant Sionna. Baby with pizza face.

(SOUND: chickens, laugh, car engine dream)

He wakes. Sionna beside him.

NARRATOR
You had a baby. Face was a pizza.

SIONNA
(laughing soft)
Means you're giving birth to a new idea. Fast delivery guarantee?

INT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

Bob enters, pupils wide, animated. Narrator at counter.

(SOUND: door, oven, street)

BOB
Little brother. Nick barely survived. Cops hounding him. Might lock him up.

NARRATOR
He accidentally killed one person. I'm not okay with Nick. I'm not okay with Ma dead. I'm not okay with anything.

Bob backs up, hands raised.

INT. SIONNA DUPLEX - NIGHT

Sionna massages Narrator's neck. Rose and cookie smell.

(SOUND: soft knead, distant traffic)

SIONNA
Someone said not to trip over things behind you. This is your life. Your play. I love you.

INT. HOSPITAL / NICK'S APARTMENT / BUS - DAY

Nick POV. Hospital gown then street clothes. Rants alone. Bus full of bag ladies, kids with green hair. He smokes under no-smoking sign.

(SOUND: bus hiss, coughs, his lighter)

NICK (V.O.)
That little bitch. It's all her fault. He had one job. Take care of Ma.

INT. SCHOOL CAFETERIA / SIDEWALK - DAY - FLASHBACK

Young Nick threatens curly-blonde JOHN JOHANSSON at goalpost.

YOUNG NICK
You talk to my brother again, you hang upside down till you pass out and die.

Later: Dad (blue puffy coat, red hair, fat nose) drags young Nick, belt snaps.

DAD
Lean over.

Belt hits. Young Nick cries into pillow.

(SOUND: belt crack, child sob, door slam)

INT. BUS / EXT. SIONNA DUPLEX - DAY

Nick in heavy coat, switchblade in pocket, rides bus route 10. Gray cold. Knocks. Sionna opens crack. Gray cat. He steps in, no seat.

(SOUND: bus brakes, knock hard, door creak, cat)

NICK
You're the reason I'm probably going to jail.

SIONNA
Let's talk rationally.

He backs her to hallway wall, left hand on her neck, right flicks switchblade open. Edge to collarbone.

(SOUND: blade snick, her squeak, his breath)

NICK
Break it off tonight. Cash in one week. Or this blade. Friends who don't care if your parents live.

Front door thunks open.

NARRATOR
Holy shit, Nick, what the fuck are you doing?

Struggle. Knife sinks. Red flies. Sionna sinks making weird noises. Nick elbows Narrator, wipes blade on her shirt, flees.

(SOUND: door bang, scuffle, wet knife sound, her gasp, running feet)

INT. SIONNA DUPLEX HALLWAY - DAY

Narrator rips flannel, brown camisole wet. Stacks hands on left shoulder wound. Blood everywhere on him. Phone 911. Sirens. Paramedics. Cop.

(SOUND: his shout, siren approach, radio crackle, monitor beep start)

NARRATOR
My brother stabbed her. Sionna. Nick Olson. He ran.

Paramedics work. Beeping good. She lives. Shoulder and lung.

INT. SIONNA DUPLEX - DAY

Months later. February. Sionna in loose shirt, foam pillow under left arm, blank zombie stare, eyes without light. Narrator opposite.

(SOUND: clock, distant traffic, her shallow breath)

NARRATOR
Are you depressed? I feel like you're blaming me.

SIONNA
I can't feel me. I can't feel myself anymore. As long as there is any doubt, I can't go on with you. Maybe a break. Mom and Dad's.

NARRATOR
Stay. We can work it out.

She walks to bedroom. He leaves for work early. Free fall.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
My fingers slipped off the rocky cliff. I had no one now.

EXT. SEATTLE BELLTOWN STREET - DAY

Seven years later. Gray blanket sky. Mist. Narrator (filled-out shoulders, quality sweater, Seattle rain jacket, calmer eyes) walks. Dogs. Keyboard player with felt hat. Tony's Boy's Pizza under medical building next to theater.

(SOUND: light rain, ferry horn distant, keyboard disco-jazz)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Many years. Pain and growth. Thanks to Tony I had my own chain. Tony's Boy's Pizza. Still delivered. GPS now.

INT. LOWELL'S / PIKE PLACE MARKET - DAY

Window table, Puget Sound, ferry, cruise ship. Cinnamon roll. Tourists. Narrator stares, remembering blood hallway.

(SOUND: market crowd, ferry horn, plate set down)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Seven years of hatred for my brother. Wounds stubborn as a fly on watermelon.

INT. SEATTLE YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

Candles. Citrus lavender. Gratefulness theme. Narrator on mat among ten others.

(SOUND: soft chant, breath, mat roll)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Shakespeare: things neither good nor bad but thinking makes it so. I still felt the void. And sorrow for him.

INT. SEATTLE BELLTOWN APARTMENT - NIGHT

Warm. Bozo the orange-and-white cat on couch. Narrator writes in notebook after dream.

(SOUND: rain on window, pen scratch, cat purr)

Dream residue: Indian sage, dirty pizza box on green road.

SAGE (V.O.)
Stop judging. All things illusion. You create your own reality. Start living the truth.

Narrator smiles, alive.

NARRATOR
I have to see Nick. I have to forgive.

He books a flight on laptop.

EXT. KIRK STREET BRICK APARTMENT / ALLEY - DAY

Same timeless brick. Dumpsters now green-brown. Hispanic couple with stroller. Narrator (Seattle layers) stares, hollow.

(SOUND: street, stroller wheel, his quiet breath)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nothing changed. I had been his serf. Now a grown man.

EXT. OLD COSMETOLOGY SCHOOL - DAY

Former pizza parlor. Weeds. Sign: Central Cosmetology, pink scissors, powder puff. Dark inside. Same window.

(SOUND: wind in weeds, distant car)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Tony's son sold it. I laughed. I used to think owning one place was the top.

INT. DELI - DAY

Narrator with Reuben. Bob (graying thinning hair, thicker waist, carved lines, factory clothes) at window with greasy burger. Looks up.

(SOUND: deli chatter, plate clink, his cackle)

BOB
Holy shit. Is that you, bro? You came back, you son of a bitch.

Bear hug. Patrons stare.

BOB
You look so grown up. Big shoulders. I took over his apartment. Factory now. Proud of you. You got out.

NARRATOR
I need to let the shit go.

BOB
Let it go. You're a wise man.

EXT. SIONNA DUPLEX - DAY

Grayer. Naked porch, no chairs, no plants. Weeds in gravel. Narrator in car, engine off.

(SOUND: engine tick, kids on bikes distant, his exhale)

Memory: bamboo rockers, Smiley cat, air kiss.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I love her so much. I just want her to be happy. Leave her alone. Move forward.

He starts the engine.

INT. GREEN BAY CORRECTIONAL INSTITUTE VISIT BOOTH - DAY

Oppressive brick outside. Metal detectors. Waiting. Double-locked doors. Plexiglass, yellow receiver, Formica. Narrator sits. Guard nearby.

(SOUND: locks clank, amplified voices, phone pickup)

Nick enters opposite: orange jumpsuit, scrawny, graying thinning hair, forehead wrinkles, watery bagged eyes, older. Weak smile. They stare.

NICK
Hi, bro. Glad you came.

NARRATOR
How are you holding up?

NICK
Not a cakewalk. Looking good for early. Parole hearing in two years maybe.

NARRATOR
I'm here to tell you some things. Just listen.

He leans in.

NARRATOR
You're an asshole. You killed my mother drunk. You held me down my whole life. You tried to kill the love of my life and drove her away.

(SOUND: his breath, distant door lock, Nick's quiet cry start)

NARRATOR
I forgive you. It sets me free. Releases your control forever.

Nick's tears flow onto Formica.

NICK
You're so right. I am a huge asshole. I feel so bad. You're more than I ever was. I deserve to live in here.

GUARD
Time's almost up.

NARRATOR
I want you to get better. I still believe in you. I still love you for being my brother.

NICK
I love you too. What are you doing now?

NARRATOR
I own a pizza chain in Seattle. Expanding. You always said I couldn't. I did it, Nick.

NICK
President Olson. That's fucking amazing. I am so proud of you.

Guard ends it. Nick shuffled away. Narrator sits with yellow receiver, light.

EXT. AIRPORT / INT. TERMINAL - DAY

Narrator walks maze of people, floating. Mixed nuts, tea. Boarding queue. Scans pass.

(SOUND: airport murmur, boarding call, scanner beep)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Weight of thirty years gone. Free of fear, guilt, sadness, judgment. I send him love and hope. I will never see him again by choice.

He looks at the name on the pass. Whispers.

NARRATOR
That's me. President Peter Olson.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
This has been Nick and me.

FADE OUT.

THE END